course: biology
lesson: 303
title: Parrots of the world: macaws, cockatoos, African greys, lorikeets and the cheeky kea
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: There are about 400 kinds of parrot, most of them in warm places, with the most kinds in Australia and South America. All have a hooked beak and feet with two toes forward and two back.
explain: The biggest are the macaws of Central and South American forests; a hyacinth macaw is about a metre long and cracks palm nuts so hard that people need a hammer.
explain: Parrots are among the cleverest birds. Cockatoos in Sydney learned to flip open wheelie bins, a cockatoo called Snowball danced in time to pop music, and in New Zealand the kea pulls rubber strips off cars just to find out what is underneath.
explain: Think of a parrot climbing its cage: beak, foot, beak, foot. It uses its beak like a third foot and holds its food in one foot like a hand.
explain: Sadly, more than a quarter of parrot kinds are threatened, mainly by forest loss and by people catching them as pets.
fact: Macaws are the biggest parrots; the hyacinth macaw is about a metre long from beak to tail and its beak cracks palm nuts so hard people need a hammer; macaws live in the forests of Central and South America.
fact: In Peru, hundreds of macaws and other parrots gather at riverbanks called clay licks to eat clay; some scientists think it protects them from poisons in seeds, others think it gives them salt, and it is still argued.
fact: Cockatoos raise their crest feathers when excited or alarmed; in Sydney, cockatoos learned to flip open wheelie bins and the trick spread from suburb to suburb.
fact: A cockatoo called Snowball became famous for moving in time to music; keeping a beat is rare in animals, and a study reported he invented more than a dozen dance moves.
fact: African greys are famous mimics that can learn many words and sounds; in the wild they copy the calls of their flock, and since 2016 trade in wild-caught African greys has been banned worldwide.
fact: Lorikeets have brush-tipped tongues for licking nectar and pollen from flowers, and they are noisy visitors to gardens in eastern Australia.
fact: The kea is a big green mountain parrot of New Zealand, one of the very few parrots that live among snowy mountains; its curiosity helps it find food in a harsh place.
fact: Keas can solve puzzles with several steps and work as a team; a study reported they can judge chances; the kea is endangered, with only a few thousand left.
fact: Parrots have two toes pointing forward and two back on each foot, so they can grip branches and hold food like a hand; many parrots prefer their left foot, studies report.
fact: A parrot uses its beak like a third foot for climbing; its upper beak is hinged to the skull, and its thick muscular tongue helps it handle food and shape sounds.
fact: More than a quarter of parrot kinds are threatened, mainly by forest loss and the pet trade; escaped ring-necked parakeets now live wild in cities like London and Madrid.
exercise: Which are the biggest parrots? | Macaws | Lorikeets | Budgies | Little keas
exercise: About how long is a hyacinth macaw from beak to tail? | About a metre | About 10 centimetres | About 3 metres | About 30 centimetres
exercise: Why do macaws gather to eat clay from riverbanks? | Scientists still argue: perhaps against seed poisons, perhaps for salt | Because they have run out of every other kind of food in the forest for good | To build mud nests on the riverbank | To sharpen their beaks on the stones
exercise: What does it usually mean when a cockatoo raises its crest? | It is excited or alarmed | It is fast asleep and dreaming | It is about to lay an egg | It is feeling cold and wet
exercise: What trick did Sydney cockatoos learn and spread? | Flipping open wheelie bins | Opening car doors with keys | Turning on garden taps | Ringing doorbells for food
exercise: What made the cockatoo Snowball famous? | Moving in time to music | Talking in full sentences | Flying around the whole world | Living for 300 years or more
exercise: Why do wild African greys copy sounds? | To copy the calls of their flock and fit in | To trick people into feeding them sunflower seeds | To scare snakes away | Because they have no voice of their own
exercise: What happened to trade in wild African greys in 2016? | It was banned worldwide | It doubled in size that year | It became legal everywhere | Nothing, it was never controlled
exercise: What is special about a lorikeet's tongue? | It has a brush tip for licking nectar and pollen | It is as hard as a nut-cracker for cracking big palm nuts | It is forked like a snake's | It is sticky for catching flies
exercise: Where does the kea live? | Among the snowy mountains of New Zealand | In the hot dry deserts of northern Mexico | In the rainforests of Peru | On the ice of Antarctica, with penguins
exercise: Why does a kea pull things apart, like a car's rubber strips? | Curiosity helps it find food in a harsh place | It hates cars and wants them gone from its mountains | It eats rubber as its main food | It builds nests from car parts
exercise: Which is true about keas in puzzle tests? | They solve puzzles with several steps and work as a team | They cannot solve any puzzle at all, however simple | They only solve puzzles when music is playing nearby | They give up after one try
exercise: How are a parrot's toes arranged? | Two forward and two back | Three forward and one back | All four pointing forward | Two forward and none back
exercise: What does a parrot use as a «third foot» when climbing? | Its beak | Its long tail | Its tongue | Its crest
exercise: What are the main threats to wild parrots? | Forest loss and the pet trade | Too much fruit in the forest | Snow in the rainforest | Clay licks running out
exercise: Where do escaped ring-necked parakeets now live wild? | In cities like London and Madrid | Only in Antarctica | Only on remote Pacific islands | Nowhere, they all died
test: Count them: a parrot sits on a perch. How many of its toes point backwards? | 4 | 2 | 0 | 8
test: Count them: a macaw climbs using both feet and its beak. How many «hands» is it using? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 1
test: In the film Rio, Blu the macaw lives in a cosy home far from his forest. What is true for real macaws? | Wild macaws belong in forests; catching them for pets made some kinds rare | Macaws are happiest in small cages, where they feel safe from the eagles of the forest | All macaws come from snowy mountains | Macaws were never caught for pets
test: In Aladdin, the parrot Iago chats with people as if he understands every word. What is true? | Parrots are brilliant mimics, but they do not hold real chats like people | Parrots understand and speak every language, if they hear it long enough | Parrots cannot copy any sound | Only cartoon parrots can copy sounds
test: «my uncle wants a parrot caught in the jungle, it's cheaper innit» What is best? | Choose one bred by people; catching wild ones harms wild flocks | Wild-caught ones are better pets, because they are stronger and cleverer | It does not matter where it comes from | Wild parrots love to be caught
test: What would happen if a lorikeet only had hard seeds to eat? | It would struggle, because its tongue is built for nectar and pollen | It would crack them easily like a macaw | Its tongue would slowly harden and turn into a second beak | It would start eating clay only
test: A friend says the bin-opening trick spread because each cockatoo was born knowing it. What is more likely? | Birds learned it by watching others | They all read the same book | The bins were made for cockatoos | Only one cockatoo opened all the bins
test: «why's that green parrot in the snow, is it lost?» You are in the New Zealand mountains. What is it? | Probably a kea, a parrot that lives among snowy mountains | A lost pet that will freeze tonight | A macaw on holiday | A lorikeet looking for nectar
test: «dad reckons the macaws at the riverbank are eating mud cos they're starving» What is best? | They eat clay on purpose; scientists think it may block poisons or give salt | Yes, they are starving, because every fruit tree in the forest has died | They are building a dam | They are drinking, not eating
test: A parrot picks up a nut in one foot and lifts it to its beak. Which foot is it most likely to use? | Often the left, as many parrots prefer it | Always the right, no parrot is left-footed | Neither, parrots never use feet for food | Both feet at once, every time
test: Why might people need a hammer for a nut a hyacinth macaw cracks with its beak? | The macaw's beak is extremely strong | The macaw softens the nut by singing | Macaws only eat soft nuts | The macaw drops it from the sky first
exam: Identify the group of parrots that includes the largest members of the parrot family. | The macaws of Central and South America | The lorikeets of eastern Australia | The ring-necked parakeets of Europe | The keas of New Zealand
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the arrangement of the toes of a parrot? | Two toes are directed forward and two backward upon each foot | Three toes are directed forward and one backward upon each foot | All toes are directed forward upon each foot | The toes are fused into a single grasping claw
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the international trade in wild-caught African grey parrots. | Such trade has been prohibited worldwide since the year two thousand and sixteen | Such trade has been actively encouraged since the year two thousand and sixteen | Such trade has never been subject to any regulation | Such trade is restricted solely to the continent of Africa
exam: Ascertain the function of the brush-tipped tongue of the lorikeet. | The collection of nectar and pollen from flowers | The cracking of hard palm nuts | The capture of flying insects in the air | The production of mimicked sounds and human speech
